| normal command pattern | `GET /api/cmd?op=<name>` — one route, one result shape `{ok,op,msg}` |
| load bitmaps + animations + editor, send with one command | Bitmap tab; `POST /api/bmp` takes 288 bytes of hex |
| drop now-playing, use random | gone; fullscreen uses random phrases |
| setText with scroll options | Text tab — scroll is a checkbox, not a fact |
| icons on setText | `setTextStyled(text, icon, src, fmt)` — new in the library |
| power on/off | Text tab |
| clock: input + browser | Text tab, HHMM field + browser-clock button |
| showMenu, highlight 1/2 | Menus tab |
| info menu, per-row running | Menus tab, a scroll checkbox per row |
| popup open/close with icon select | Text tab, same icon set as setText |
| fullscreen, random full width | Text tab |
| confirm box with Yes/No | Menus tab — **see the caveat below** |
| change panel family | Settings tab (a BOOT choice, stored in NVS) |
| wire log with export | Wire tab, exports `.tsv` |
| test menu for buttons | Keys tab — live, decoded, nothing emulated |
| overall settings page | Settings tab |
| tabs, fast, embedded | six tabs, one page, no external requests |

## Library additions (display-related only)

* `affa::keyName(Key)` / `affa::edgeName(KeyEdge)`
* `CarminatDisplay::setTextStyled(text, icon, srcIcon, fmt)` — the four documented `0x77`
  header bytes, previously hard-coded
* `carminat::kButtonsNone/Ok/YesNo`, `kButtonLabelLen`, `kSelectModeBox/List`

Earlier in the session, also landed: `enqueueExternal`, `enqueueSplit`, `showNavBitmap`,
`navTick`, `showMenuN`, `selectMenuItem`, `lastRendered()` / `lastRenderedMs()`.

## NOT DONE — read before assuming

**`showConfirmBox` is a one-button OK box.** It emits `21 05 00 00 01 49`; byte 4 is the
button count, confirmed on four OEM captures with 0, 1 and 2 buttons. The real Yes/No form is
`21 05 01 00 02 49` with two 6-byte labels before the body, and **the library has no builder
for it**. The console's "select 0 / select 1" buttons send `29 05 <n>`, which is how a box
moves its selection — on a one-button box only index 0 exists, so index 1 doing nothing is
the expected answer, not a fault. The console says this where the buttons are.

## The standing caveat

This panel ACKs everything. All 23 ops returning `ok` proves the transport worked and
nothing else. Every button still needs confirming against the glass — that is what the
Keys and Wire tabs are for.
